00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:08.880
god is dead god remains dead and we have&nbsp;
killed him friedrich nietzsche wrote in 1882&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:16.480
to understand what the german philosopher meant&nbsp;
and what he thought of men morality and society&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:22.480
as a whole we collaborated with professor&nbsp;
stephen hicks on this sprout special series

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:37.040
part one god is dead for thousands&nbsp;
of years humans have been religious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:41.760
but in the modern world religion has&nbsp;
become a shadow of its former self&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:49.200
nietzsche's dramatic phrase god is dead is meant&nbsp;
to capture the shocking quality of this revelation

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:56.160
for those raised religiously&nbsp;
religion personalized the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.160 --> 00:01:01.360
it gave them a sense that the world had a&nbsp;
purpose and that they were part of a larger plan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:09.440
it gave them the comfort that despite appearances&nbsp;
we are all equal and cared for and that upon death&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:14.560
instead of a cold grave a possible&nbsp;
happily ever after ending awaits

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:23.040
but in the modern world we find it increasingly&nbsp;
hard to believe those values we have seen the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:29.200
dramatic rise of science which has offered&nbsp;
less comfortable answers to questions religion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:35.920
traditionally had a monopoly on we have thrown off&nbsp;
the shackles of feudalism with its unquestioning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:41.920
acceptance of authority and have become more&nbsp;
individualistic and naturalistic in our thinking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:52.240
but in historical time all of this has happened&nbsp;
very quickly for millennia we have been religious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:57.840
but come the 19th century even the average&nbsp;
man had heard that religion may have reached&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:04.000
the end of its journey for most of us even&nbsp;
the suggestion of this hints at a crisis

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:12.400
imagine a 13 year old who is awakened in the&nbsp;
middle of the night to be told by strangers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:20.160
that both his parents have died he is suddenly an&nbsp;
orphan for as long as he can remember his mother&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:27.840
and father were present in his life looking after&nbsp;
him and guiding him now they are gone and ready or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:35.840
not he is thrust into that world alone how does&nbsp;
the young teen handle that sudden transition

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:44.240
culturally nietzsche says we are like that young&nbsp;
teen for as long as we can remember our society&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:50.720
has relied upon god the father to look&nbsp;
after us but now suddenly we are orphaned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:56.720
we wake up one morning to discover that in&nbsp;
our heart of hearts our naively childhood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.720 --> 00:03:02.560
religious beliefs have withered so now&nbsp;
whether we like it or not a question&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:08.800
creeps into our minds how do we face the&nbsp;
prospect of a world without god and religion

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:16.080
in the 19th century says nietzsche most&nbsp;
people did not face that question well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:23.040
most people avoid the issue sensing that even to&nbsp;
raise it would be to enter dangerous territory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:30.480
life without religion is too scary to contemplate&nbsp;
so they retreat to a safety zone of belief&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:34.320
and repeat nervously the formulas&nbsp;
they have learned about faith

00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:42.080
slightly better to nietzsche but not much&nbsp;
are the socialists of the 19th century&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:49.520
socialism is on the rise and many socialists&nbsp;
accept that god is dead but then they are very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:56.240
concerned that the state take god's place and look&nbsp;
after them the mighty state will provide for us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.240 --> 00:04:04.640
and tell us what to do against the mean people of&nbsp;
the world think of it this way the judeo-christian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:10.320
tradition says this is a world of sin in&nbsp;
which the weak suffer at the hands of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:16.960
strong that we should all be selfless and serve&nbsp;
god and others especially the sick and helpless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:24.560
and that in a future ideal world heaven&nbsp;
the lion will lie down with lamb and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:30.320
inescapable power of god will bring salvation&nbsp;
to the meek and judgment to the wicked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:39.200
the socialist tradition says this is a world&nbsp;
of evil exploitation in which the strong take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:45.600
advantage of the weak but we should all be&nbsp;
selfless and sacrifice for the good of others&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:51.920
especially the needy from each according to&nbsp;
his ability to each according to his need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:59.040
and the forces of history will necessarily&nbsp;
bring about a future ideal world that will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:05.520
end all harsh competition empowering the&nbsp;
oppressed and eliminating the evil exploiters

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:15.520
both religion and socialism thus glorify weakness&nbsp;
and need both recoil from the world as it is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:22.960
tough unequal harsh both flee to an&nbsp;
imaginary future realm where they can feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.960 --> 00:05:31.760
safe both say be a nice boy be a good little&nbsp;
girl share feel sorry for the little people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:37.840
and both desperately seek someone to look&nbsp;
after them whether it be god or the state&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:46.400
so where asks nature are the men of courage&nbsp;
who is willing to stare into the abyss who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.400 --> 00:05:52.400
can stand alone on the icy mountain top who&nbsp;
can look at tiger in the eye without flinching

